$CATEGORY: PHY/U1 L1 Displacement and Velocity

::PHY-U1-L1-Q01::Which statement best distinguishes distance from displacement? {
=Distance is scalar path length; displacement is the vector from initial to final position.#Correct. Distance has magnitude only, while displacement includes direction.
~Distance always equals displacement.#Not when direction changes or the path is indirect.
~Distance includes direction; displacement does not.#The descriptions are reversed.
~Both quantities are vectors.#Distance is a scalar.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q02::A runner completes one full 400 m lap and stops at the starting line. What are the runner's distance and displacement? {
~0 m and 400 m#The traveled path is not zero.
=400 m and 0 m#Correct. The path is 400 m, but initial and final positions coincide.
~400 m and 400 m#Displacement is zero after returning to the start.
~0 m and 0 m#The runner traveled 400 m.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q03::Which quantity is a vector? {
~time#Time has magnitude only.
~distance#Distance is scalar.
=velocity#Correct. Velocity includes magnitude and direction.
~speed#Speed is scalar.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q04::A car travels 60 m east and then 20 m west. What is its displacement? {
~80 m east#That is the total distance, not displacement.
~40 m west#The net direction is east.
~80 m west#Both magnitude and direction are incorrect.
=40 m east#Correct. Taking east as positive gives 60 - 20 = 40 m east.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q05::What does the slope of a position-time graph represent? {
=velocity#Correct. Change in position divided by change in time is velocity.
~acceleration#Acceleration is the slope of a velocity-time graph.
~distance#Distance is not represented by this slope.
~elapsed time#Elapsed time is on the horizontal axis.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q06::A horizontal segment on a position-time graph indicates that the object is {
~moving with constant positive velocity.#Positive velocity would produce an upward slope.
=at rest.#Correct. Position is not changing with time.
~speeding up.#Speeding up would require a changing slope.
~moving with constant negative velocity.#Negative velocity would produce a downward slope.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q07::An object moves from x = 3 m to x = -5 m. What is its displacement? {
~8 m#This is the magnitude only and omits direction/sign.
~-2 m#Subtract the initial position from the final position.
=-8 m#Correct. Δx = -5 m - 3 m = -8 m.
~2 m#The subtraction is incorrect.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q08::Which equation gives average velocity? {
~total distance multiplied by elapsed time#Velocity is not found by multiplication.
~elapsed time divided by displacement#The ratio is inverted.
~total distance divided by elapsed time#That gives average speed.
=displacement divided by elapsed time#Correct. Average velocity is Δx/Δt.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q09::A student walks 30 m north in 20 s. What is the average velocity? {
~0.67 m/s north#This is the reciprocal relationship.
=1.5 m/s north#Correct. 30 m / 20 s = 1.5 m/s north.
~600 m/s north#Distance and time were multiplied.
~1.5 m/s#The magnitude is correct, but velocity also requires direction.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q10::Which statement correctly compares speed and velocity? {
~Both always have direction.#Speed does not have direction.
~Velocity is always positive.#Velocity may be positive, negative, or zero.
=Speed is scalar, while velocity is vector.#Correct. Velocity contains directional information.
~Speed uses displacement, while velocity uses distance.#The descriptions are reversed for average values.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q11::On a dot diagram with equal time intervals, increasingly separated dots show that the object is {
~at rest.#An object at rest would have overlapping dots.
~moving at constant speed.#Constant speed gives equal spacing.
=speeding up.#Correct. Greater distance covered each interval means increasing speed.
~slowing down.#Slowing down gives decreasing spacing.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q12::A straight position-time line with a constant negative slope represents {
~constant positive velocity.#The slope is negative.
~zero velocity.#Zero velocity would be horizontal.
~changing negative velocity.#A straight line has constant slope.
=constant negative velocity.#Correct. Constant negative slope means constant negative velocity.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q13::If east is chosen as positive, a velocity of -12 m/s means the object moves {
=west at 12 m/s.#Correct. The negative sign indicates the direction opposite east.
~east at 12 m/s.#East would be positive.
~west at -12 m/s of speed.#Speed is not negative.
~without direction.#Velocity necessarily has direction.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q14::A cyclist travels 100 m east and 100 m west in 50 s. What is the average velocity? {
~4 m/s east#This uses total distance and gives speed, not velocity.
=0 m/s#Correct. Net displacement is zero.
~2 m/s west#Net displacement is zero.
~200 m/s#The value and units reasoning are incorrect.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q15::Which graph feature shows the object with the greatest speed on a position-time graph? {
~the highest position value#Position height alone does not determine speed.
~the longest time interval#Time interval alone does not determine speed.
~the line closest to horizontal#A horizontal line represents zero speed.
=the segment with the greatest slope magnitude#Correct. Speed is the magnitude of position-time slope.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q16::A position-time graph rises linearly from 0 m at 0 s to 20 m at 4 s. What is the velocity? {
~80 m/s#This multiplies instead of divides.
~0.2 m/s#This reverses the ratio.
=5 m/s#Correct. The slope is 20 m / 4 s = 5 m/s.
~-5 m/s#The graph rises, so the velocity is positive.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q17::What does the area under a velocity-time graph represent? {
~average speed#Area does not directly give average speed.
~acceleration#Acceleration is represented by slope.
~elapsed time#Time is the horizontal dimension only.
=displacement#Correct. Signed area under velocity versus time gives displacement.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q18::An object moves at -4 m/s for 3 s. What is its displacement? {
~-1.3 m#This divides instead of multiplies.
~12 m#The magnitude is right, but the sign is missing.
=-12 m#Correct. Δx = vΔt = (-4 m/s)(3 s) = -12 m.
~-7 m#Velocity and time should be multiplied.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q19::Which statement about a reference frame is correct? {
=Motion is described relative to a chosen origin and coordinate direction.#Correct. Position and velocity depend on the stated reference frame.
~A reference frame changes an object's physical path.#It changes the description, not the actual path.
~Only stationary observers can define a reference frame.#Moving observers can also define frames.
~Direction signs are identical in every reference frame.#The coordinate choice may differ.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q20::A boat heads 3 m/s east while a current carries it 4 m/s north. What is the magnitude of its resultant velocity? {
~1 m/s#Vector magnitudes are not subtracted for perpendicular components.
~7 m/s#Perpendicular components are not simply added.
~12 m/s#The components should not be multiplied.
=5 m/s#Correct. The Pythagorean theorem gives √(3² + 4²) = 5 m/s.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q21::Why must axes on a motion graph include units? {
~Units make every line straight.#Units do not control graph shape.
=Units identify the measured quantities and allow the slope or area to be interpreted correctly.#Correct. A calculation from a graph is meaningful only with labeled quantities and units.
~Units determine whether motion is positive.#The coordinate convention determines sign.
~Units replace the need for a scale.#Both scale and units are needed.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q22::Two objects have position-time lines with equal slope magnitude but opposite signs. They have {
~the same velocity.#Their velocity directions differ.
~different speeds and the same direction.#Their speed magnitudes are equal.
=the same speed in opposite directions.#Correct. Equal slope magnitude gives equal speed; opposite signs give opposite directions.
~zero displacement.#Neither must have zero displacement.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q23::Which procedure correctly finds displacement from a sequence of one-dimensional motions? {
~Add all path lengths without signs.#That finds distance.
~Use only the longest segment.#Every segment contributes to displacement.
~Divide each segment by total time.#That does not find displacement.
=Assign signs by direction and add the signed changes in position.#Correct. Vector components in one dimension combine algebraically.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q24::A student says an object at position -6 m must be moving in the negative direction. What is the best response? {
~The student is correct because negative position means negative velocity.#Position and velocity are different quantities.
~The student is correct only if time is positive.#Positive time does not establish direction.
=The claim is unsupported; position alone does not determine velocity direction.#Correct. The object could be moving either direction or be at rest at -6 m.
~The object must be at rest.#A single position does not determine motion state.
}

::PHY-U1-L1-Q25::Which evidence most strongly supports a claim of uniform motion? {
~A curved position-time graph.#A curve means changing slope and velocity.
~Unequal spacing in equal-time dot intervals.#Unequal spacing indicates changing speed.
=A straight position-time graph and equally spaced dots at equal time intervals.#Correct. Both representations show constant velocity.
~A velocity-time graph that crosses zero repeatedly.#That shows changing direction.
}
